WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:09.700
When curl connects over HTTPS, certificate validation helps confirm that the script is communicating with the expected service through a protected channel.

2
00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:14.120
That trust check is part of the security design, not a cosmetic feature.

3
00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:20.080
Disabling verification can make a failing script appear to work, but it removes an important control.

4
00:00:20.180 --> 00:00:25.300
In automation, that is especially risky because there may be no person watching the request.

5
00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:31.480
A job may send credentials, payloads, or downloaded output through a channel it has not properly verified.

6
00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:37.040
Internal services sometimes use private certificate authorities or managed trust stores.

7
00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:40.740
That is normal, but it should be handled deliberately.

8
00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:50.360
The better pattern is to configure the appropriate trust store or CA bundle for the environment, not to bypass verification because the script runs on an internal network.

9
00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:55.040
Proxies and inspection systems also affect transport trust.

10
00:00:55.140 --> 00:01:01.140
A corporate proxy, service mesh, or inspection gateway may terminate or reissue certificates.

11
00:01:01.240 --> 00:01:08.120
Scripts should be reviewed with those network assumptions in mind so teams understand what identity is actually being trusted.

12
00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:12.800
Transport trust should be explicit in the runbook and script design.

13
00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:20.180
If a certificate changes, a CA rotates, or a proxy path changes, that should be treated as an operational event.

14
00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:25.560
Secure curl automation keeps verification enabled and manages trust intentionally.
